{"id":"c628ae5b-9685-4eba-b638-ebd3dcb20b3d","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009643,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    # build id->node map\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    # build children map\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    # initial retained set all ids\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    # sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue  # root, though already not removable\\n        # check no retained children\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        # check required symbols still represented after removal\\n        # need at least one other node with each required symbol\\n        okay = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            # count nodes with this symbol in retained, excluding current node\\n            count = 0\\n            for other_id in retained:\\n                if other_id == nid:\\n                    continue\\n                if node_by_id[other_id]['symbol'] == sym:\\n                    count += 1\\n            if count == 0:\\n                okay = False\\n                break\\n        if not okay:\\n            continue\\n        # remove\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    # sort retained ids\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that performs grammar-guided node reduction on a tree given in the payload. The payload has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. It also has required_symbols. The output should have removed_node_ids (list of string IDs in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of strings). The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process nodes in descending depth. \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to check if after removal, all required symbols are still present in the retained nodes. \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for nodes at same depth, order by ID (string). \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in the order we removed.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree. We iterate over nodes in descending depth, and within same depth, ascending ID. For each node that is removable (removable == true), we tentatively consider removing it. But first check: not root (parent null?), not a required symbol? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if node.id is root? root has parent null. Also check if node's symbol is in required_symbols? Actually \"required symbol\" refers to a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But careful: multiple nodes can have same symbol. So we must ensure that at least one node with each required symbol remains after removal. The node itself might be the only one with that symbol, so we can't remove it. Also \"a node with retained children\" – if we haven't removed its children, we cannot remove it because that would orphan them? Actually the tree is hierarchical, if we remove a node, its children become orphaned? In typical node reduction, removing a node might mean deleting the subtree. The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". I think removal means deleting that node and its subtree? Or maybe it means we can prune the node, but children are considered? The phrase \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has children that are retained (not removed), then we cannot remove the parent because that would leave children without a parent? Or maybe the reduction process removes nodes and then children are reattached? The problem context is \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar\". Typically, removal of a node means deleting that subtree entirely. So if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed. Then the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" would be redundant because if we remove a node, its children are not retained. But the condition says \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" meaning that if some children are still in the tree (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent. So that implies that children may be removed independently? Actually in a tree reduction, you might remove some subtrees, but if you remove a parent, you cannot keep children because they are part of the subtree. So the condition likely means that you cannot remove a node if any of its children are still present (i.e., not yet removed). Because if you remove children first, then the parent becomes leaf and can be removed. So the order: process deeper nodes first, so children before parents. When we consider a node, if it has any children that are still retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove it. Only after all its children have been removed (or are already considered removed) can we consider removing the node itself. Since we process in descending depth, children are deeper, so they are considered earlier. So when we get to a parent, its children may have been removed already. So condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" is automatically satisfied if we only consider removing nodes after processing all descendants, and only if those descendants are removed or never removable? But we also have the condition that we cannot remove required symbols or root, so those nodes will remain. So children of a required symbol cannot be removed if they are required? Actually children are separate nodes. The algorithm: iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then ID ascending. For each node:\n- If node is not removable, skip.\n- If node is root (parent null?), skip.\n- If node's symbol is in required_symbols? Actually \"Never remove a required symbol\" – but multiple nodes can have same symbol. The requirement is that after removal, all required symbols must still be represented somewhere. So if this node is the only node with that symbol, then removing it would violate required_symbols, so we cannot remove it. But if there are multiple nodes with that symbol, we could remove one as long as at least one remains. However, the condition \"never remove a required symbol\" might be interpreted as never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, regardless of duplicates. But that seems too strict and would never remove any node with a required symbol even if duplicates. The problem says \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"a required symbol\", not \"a node whose symbol is required\". That ambiguous but likely means \"a node that is the only provider of a required symbol\"? Actually, in grammar-guided reduction, you cannot remove a node that contains a required nonterminal. Perses typically requires that at least one node with each required nonterminal remains. The phrase \"required symbol\" might refer to the symbol that must be present in the tree. So if a node has a required symbol, it cannot be removed if it is the only one. But the condition is stated as \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This seems like a rule: do not remove a node that is a required symbol (i.e., its symbol is in required_symbols). But that would conflict with the examples? In the fixtures, there are nodes with symbol \"s1\" that have removable=true. For example, in first case, n4 has symbol \"s1\", removable true. required_symbols include \"s1\". If the rule is never remove any node with required symbol, then n4 cannot be removed. But maybe the algorithm allows removal if there is another node with same symbol. The problem says \"all required symbols remain represented\" which is a weaker condition. The phrase \"never remove a required symbol\" might be shorthand for \"never remove a node if it is the only node providing a required symbol\". Given the objective \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\", we should implement the condition: before tentatively removing, check if after removal, for every required symbol, there is at least one retained node with that symbol. That is the safe interpretation and matches typical Perses.\n\nAlso \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sorting order for processing: first by depth descending, then by id ascending. For nodes at same depth, alphabetical order of id? The ids are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\". So string comparison is fine.\n\nThe output: removed_node_ids: list of IDs in the order they were removed (presumably the order we process and decide to remove). retained_node_ids: sorted list of IDs of all nodes that remain after all removal decisions.\n\nWe must not mutate input case. So we need to work on a copy of nodes list, or track retained set.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case payload. It has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list.\n2. Build a lookup: node_id -> node dict. Also build children mapping: parent_id -> list of child ids (or set).\n3. Determine order: sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n4. Initialize retained_set = set of all node ids initially.\n5. removed_list = [].\n6. For each node in order:\n   - if node['removable'] is False: skip.\n   - if node['parent'] is None (root): skip (though root is marked removable false anyway).\n   - Check \"retained children\": any child in retained_set? Since we process children first (deeper), children may have been removed already. But if any child is still retained, we cannot remove parent. So: if node has children that are in retained_set, skip.\n   - Check required symbols: for each sym in required_symbols, check if there is at least one retained node (excluding current node? but we are testing removal) with that symbol. If there is another node with same symbol that is retained, then it's okay. If the current node is the only one, skip.\n   - If both conditions pass, then we tentatively remove: removed_list.append(node['id']), and retained_set.remove(node['id']). Note: removing the node may affect required symbols condition for subsequent nodes? But since we already checked that other nodes provide the required symbols, it's fine. Also, removing a node might affect the \"retained children\" condition for its ancestors? Actually ancestors are processed later because they have shallower depth. So when we later process an ancestor, we will check its children. Since we removed this node, that child is no longer in retained_set, so ancestor might become removable if all its children are removed. That's correct.\n7. After processing all nodes, get retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained_set)).\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nNote: required_symbols include \"root\" symbol? In examples, required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]. The root node has symbol \"root\". That node is not removable. So fine.\n\nNeed to ensure that we properly build children mapping. parent field is string or null.\n\nEdge case: nodes may have no parent? Only root has parent null.\n\nAlso, must sort retained_node_ids. Use list sort.\n\nNow produce the implementation as Python source string inside JSON under \"implementation\".\n\nWrite the function. Use only standard library. No imports needed.\n\nWe'll output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as string containing Python code.\n\nCareful with escaping quotes and newlines in JSON string.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2139,"completion_tokens":2578,"total_tokens":4717,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2143},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":91},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}